    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter tonight\u2019s slate with **Yordan Alvarez** (73.41) serving as our anchor; he faces a favorable matchup against Mahle and benefits significantly from a top-two order slot and an elite xwOBA profile. Closely trailing is **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (68.04), whose ability to exploit a right-handed matchup and favorable park factors makes him our premier source for stolen base upside despite facing the stout Burns. **Christian Yelich** (66.21) also provides a high-ceiling play against the soft-tossing Povich, as the park factors in his favor help mitigate the inherent risk of a lefty-on-lefty platoon disadvantage. **Trea Turner** (65.10) remains a core engine for the lineup, as his slot in the two-hole against Thornton offers a substantial efficiency boost that offsets his minor penalty for facing a high-delivery-speed catcher.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Hector Rodriguez** (54.46), who sits just outside our optimal configuration. While he offers solid platoon leverage against Holmes, his projected efficiency fell narrowly behind the surging **Chase Meidroth** (61.27), whose strong SP-skill matchup against Sears earned him the nod in the utility-adjacent slot. We are also monitoring the bottom of the order with caution; while **Lars Nootbaar** (56.83) earns a strong platoon boost, he draws a challenging assignment against Gerrit Cole, whose command represents the highest volatility risk in our lineup today. Conversely, **Junior Caminero** (57.19) maintains a marginal-to-strong floor despite a neutral matchup, providing enough stability to avoid a benching while we look for a spark in the late-game frames. We have opted to rest **Caleb Durbin** (40.79) as his score dipped below our comfort threshold against Seymour, further highlighting the Zebras' commitment to preserving daily game caps by prioritizing elite sprint speed and high-efficiency plate appearances over volume.",
